“The torch passes to the open source recomp and emulation projects,” the gang says. CyberLeek also appears to have settled for merely asking for donations to the group’s meme coin after its previous efforts to drive up the coin's market cap flopped. One community GTA 6 recompilation project, Leonida, has been examining the leaked executable files. Traffic has been so high that the group’s chat server on Discord malfunctioned, appearing to crash. However, an admin later posted: “It will take at least a week for us to get somewhere noticeable to the point where you can play on your pc.” To avoid the project from being taken down, the admin added: “We will not be giving assets whatsoever; you will need to download the game yourself.”"This .exe file does NOT RUN THE GAME," an admin also posted before the leak dropped. "This will be used by the developers to work with and make the actual recomp, which will use the game files and exe to build the port."In the meantime, users in the project have been using AI coding tools and agents, including Anthropic’s Claude, to reverse-engineer and try to get the leaked game files running on Windows. “THE GAME ALMOST BOOTS. BLACK SCREEN, AUDIO CUTS OFF,” claimed one user in the Discord chat.

